WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.693
A laser cutter draws with light that's hot enough to cut. Here's what's going on inside.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:13.914
In the LC40, a tube of carbon dioxide gas makes a powerful infrared beam. You can't see it.

00:00:13.914 --> 00:00:17.720
Three mirrors bounce the beam across the machine to the laser head.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.462
A lens in the head focuses it to a tiny, very hot spot.

00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:29.069
The Ador works differently. Its laser module makes blue light right in the head, so there are no mirrors.

00:00:29.069 --> 00:00:35.371
But blue light passes straight through clear acrylic. So clear acrylic always goes on the LC40.

00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:40.801
Focus is everything. The beam is sharp at only one height.

00:00:40.801 --> 00:00:45.695
Too high or too low, and the spot gets wide and weak, and the cut gets charred.

00:00:45.695 --> 00:00:50.717
Set the height with the focus puck on the LC40, or autofocus on the Ador.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.235
One machine, three jobs.

00:00:54.235 --> 00:01:01.967
High power cuts all the way through. Lower power scores a line you can fold. And back and forth passes engrave a picture.

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:06.513
The beam burns away a thin strip called the kerf.

00:01:06.513 --> 00:01:13.114
So a square you draw at twenty millimeters comes out a tiny bit smaller. You'll measure that in Chapter 11.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.342
Small flickers that go out as the head moves on are normal.

00:01:18.342 --> 00:01:24.303
A flame that grows or stays is not. Keep the lid closed, press stop, and tell your teacher.

00:01:24.303 --> 00:01:27.959
That's why a laser is never, ever left alone while it runs.
